			<item>
				<title>tmux configuration</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/blog/2026/09-16_tmux/</link>
				<pubDate>Wed, 16 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/2026/09-16_tmux/</guid>
				<description>&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;m pretty much making this post just to have some test content for the blog section of my site lol.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It&amp;rsquo;s pretty rare that I have to reconfigure tmux since I usually just hang onto the same dot files forever, but it is always kind of a pain in the ass so I may as well log it here for future reference.  I&amp;rsquo;ve been planning on making a new website with the static site generator &amp;ldquo;Hugo&amp;rdquo; for a bit now.  I&amp;rsquo;ve been maining a hackintosh since ~2018 and for the past few years I&amp;rsquo;ve just been letting it fall apart.  I knew hackintosh was gonna die some day and since I generally refuse to update my OS ever if possible, I couldn&amp;rsquo;t update the &amp;ldquo;command line tools&amp;rdquo; to install Hugo without updating the whole damn OS.  I decided to say fuck it and just install Linux and Windows on some dual boot type shit.  So now after about a week copying multiple TBs of data from APFS &amp;gt; ExFat &amp;gt; NTFS and configuring Debian-stable (I will avoid updating my shit at all costs), I&amp;rsquo;ve finally got Hugo going.  I use vim as my main text editor because I want to pretend I know what I&amp;rsquo;m doing, so this means getting tmux to stay organized while I mess with Hugo.  To avoid putting my NVME drive with hackintosh installed back into my PC to fetch my old tmux config, I decided to just make a new one.  My last tmux config I made in probably 2014 was likely a mess of other people&amp;rsquo;s configs spliced together.  I&amp;rsquo;m starting fresh and haven&amp;rsquo;t made a tmux config in a while, so why not.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
